sis and treatment in TCM. [Data: Entities (626); Relationships (17959, 17960)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"急性肠炎 is associated with gastrointestinal symptoms including nausea and vomiting\",\n            \"explanation\": \"急性肠炎 (acute enteritis) is characterized by prominent gastrointestinal symptoms such as diarrhea, abdominal pain, bloating, and nausea with vomiting. Clinical documentation confirms that all observed cases presented with these features, though without electrolyte or acid-base imbalances, and were identified as bacterial in origin. This symptom profile aids in differential diagnosis and supports targeted antimicrobial or supportive care interventions. The inclusion of this condition in the TCM-related dataset suggests integration of biomedical diagnoses within traditional frameworks. [Data: Entities (974); Relationships (18058)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Multiple TCM syndromes share common contraindications for warming herbs\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Several TCM diagnostic patterns—including 外感风热, 温热, 实热内炽, 阴虚火旺, and 血虚血热—are consistently flagged as contraindications for both 丁香 and 石菖蒲. This reflects a core TCM principle: warming or acrid herbs should be avoided in conditions dominated by heat, whether external (e.g., wind-heat) or internal (e.g., yin deficiency fire). The repeated appearance of these syndromes across contraindication statements emphasizes a unified theoretical approach to herb safety, reinforcing the need for pattern-based prescribing to prevent iatrogenic harm. [Data: Relationships (6924, 5382, 5376, 5379, 5381)]\"\n        }\n    ],\n    \"rating\": 6.5,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The community poses a moderate-to-high impact due to the presence of clinically significant herb-contraindication relationships and metabolic interactions that could affect patient safety if misapplied.\"\n}", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": null, "audio": null, "funct